= BITwiki =

'''BITwiki is an interdisciplinary encyclopedia for knowledge that can be read, questioned, connected, and reused.''' It brings together clear accounts of subjects, their supporting sources, meaningful relationships, and differing interpretations. Biology, history, mathematics, philosophy, engineering, the arts, and other subjects stand on their own merits; no topic needs a computational interpretation to belong.

== Explore knowledge ==

Portals are reading routes into existing material, not a hierarchy of what knowledge matters most. A subject may be relevant to several routes.

* '''Natural sciences and life:''' [[Portal:Science|Science]] · [[Portal:Biology|Biology]] · [[Portal:Chemistry|Chemistry]] · [[Portal:Physics|Physics]] · [[Portal:Medicine|Medicine]]
* '''Mathematics, computing, and technology:''' [[Portal:Mathematics|Mathematics]] · [[Portal:Computer science|Computer science]] · [[Portal:Engineering|Engineering]] · [[Portal:Electronics|Electronics]] · [[Portal:Technology|Technology]]
* '''Mind, language, and ideas:''' [[Portal:Cognitive science|Cognitive science]] · [[Portal:Linguistics|Linguistics]] · [[Portal:Philosophy|Philosophy]]
* '''Interdisciplinary approaches:''' [[Portal:Systems science|Systems science]] · [[Portal:Energy|Energy]] · [[Portal:Bioinformatics|Bioinformatics]]

[[BITwiki:Navigation|How navigation works]] explains portals, categories, ordinary links, semantic relationships, and the difference between them. Use [[Special:AllPages|all pages]] or [[Special:Categories|categories]] for broader browsing.

== Begin with a subject ==

An article should explain its subject before discussing how the wiki organizes it. These existing topics offer different ways to begin:

* [[Causality]] — how causal explanation differs from mere association, and why interpretations may disagree.
* [[Cell membrane]] — a biological structure studied through composition, transport, and regulation.
* [[Algorithm]] — a method of computation with mathematical and practical treatments.

Follow relevant links from the account to its methods, sources, and neighboring subjects. A model of a phenomenon and the phenomenon itself are not automatically the same knowledge object.

== Read at the appropriate depth ==

Where depth-specific explanations exist, BITwiki can offer three presentations of the '''same subject'''. For example, read [[Causality/Simple|Causality — Simple]] for an initial explanation, [[Causality|Causality — Core]] for the general account, or [[Causality/Technical|Causality — Technical]] for more advanced treatment.

Simple, Core, and Technical are reading surfaces, not three competing subjects. Not every topic requires all three. Open problems and frontier research belong within a suitable account rather than constituting an automatic fourth depth. See [[BITwiki:Readable depth and localization]].

== Examine what an account claims ==

Useful knowledge requires more than an assertion or a collection of links. A reader should be able to distinguish the subject from a model of it, an observation from an interpretation, and a supported claim from one that remains uncertain or disputed.

* [[BITwiki:Evidence|Evidence]] and [[BITwiki:Sources|Sources]] — inspect what supports a claim.
* [[BITwiki:Epistemics|Epistemics]], [[BITwiki:Epistemic status|Epistemic status]], and [[BITwiki:Uncertainty|Uncertainty]] — understand justification and its limits.
* [[BITwiki:Provenance|Provenance]] — trace the origins and transformations of a claim.
* [[BITwiki:Relationships|Relationships]] — distinguish ordinary navigation from explicit semantic assertions.

A link does not prove a relationship, and an infobox or template does not establish a claim's truth.

== How knowledge is organized and reused ==

BITwiki aims to keep each article intelligible as a whole while making useful parts easier to find and maintain.

* [[BITwiki:Page format|Page format]] describes coherent subject writing and editorial review.
* [[BITwiki:Book Matter|Book Matter]] names the different jobs material performs: explanation, evidence, argument, example, method, reference, and other roles. These are not compulsory headings or separate page types.
* [[BITwiki:Transclusion|Transclusion]] describes when one maintained source should actually be included elsewhere. A contextual summary and link are often more appropriate than copying or embedding prose.
* [[BITwiki:Ontology|Ontology]] explains knowledge-object identity and justified relations; [[BITwiki:Organization|Organization]] explains the wider architecture.

This page is a '''navigation and orientation surface''', not another canonical copy of the pages it introduces. It links to their maintained explanations instead of transcluding whole articles or assigning their semantic identity to the Main Page.

== Foundations and purpose ==

Three complementary writings explain the undertaking:

* [[BITwiki:Manifesto|Manifesto]] — why BITwiki exists and what motivates the project.
* [[BITwiki:Four foundations|The Four Foundations]] — '''Ontology, Epistemology, Axiology, and Praxeology''': questions of being, knowing, valuing, and acting.
* [[BITwiki:Constitution|Constitution]] — BITwiki's canonical founding commitments and intellectual freedoms.

[[BITwiki:Foundations|Foundations]] explains how the writings relate. The four philosophical foundations are distinct from the four conditions examined in the Manifesto. Earlier Charters are historical sources, not competing constitutional authorities.

== Contribute and improve ==

Corrections, additional evidence, clearer explanations, justified cross-links, and substantive new subjects all matter. A missing topic is not an invitation to create an empty article or expose an internal research backlog.

* [[BITwiki:Contributing|Contributing]] — what to improve and how to assess a new subject.
* [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol|Editorial protocol]] — research, review, and accountable publication principles.
* [[Special:RecentChanges|Recent changes]] · [[Special:NewPages|New pages]]

A proposed contribution pathway is not necessarily an implemented submission interface. Publication and permissions remain separate from a reader's ability to explore the corpus.

== About BITwiki and its ecosystem ==

[[BITwiki:Ecosystem|BITwiki, BIThub, and BITCORE]] have distinct intended roles: durable knowledge, collaboration, and agent-supported reasoning or orchestration. Their conceptual relationship does not by itself establish an automated integration.

[[BITwiki:Source lineage|Source lineage]] documents how original material and subsequent transformations can be traced. Source history and project operations are separate from the reader-facing account of a subject.

'''This is a developing knowledge corpus.''' Sources, interpretations, semantic representations, and technical capabilities should be examined on their own evidence; revision remains part of the work.

